areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.182c1c0ea2959b4257dcf57d2b97131c9d757412.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.182c1c0ea2959b4257dcf57d2b97131c9d757412.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued October 29 at 10:53AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nLow pressure begins to dig southward from the PACNW on Friday,\ntapping into a fetch of Pacific moisture that will evolve into an\natmospheric river streaming into the Desert Southwest this weekend.\nShowers and thunderstorms develop on Friday and Saturday across the\nregion. Lighter rainfall totals Friday and Saturday will reflect,\ninitially lower, moisture. However, a few thunderstorms could produce\nimpactful downpours on saturated soils left over from last weekend's\nhistoric rainfall.\n\nDeeper moisture develops Saturday night and early Sunday. This is\nexpected to produce heavier and more widespread rainfall across\nsoutheast UT and southwest CO Sunday and Monday, mostly below 10000\nfeet. While rivers are forecast to remain below action stage, a\ngrowing concern lies in the potential for runoff around saturated\nhillsides in the region. This could produce rock, mud, and debris\nflows onto roadways, as well as flash flood prone burn scars, washes,\nslot canyons, and arroyos. Early estimates predict an area of 1 to 2\ninches of new rainfall for portions of southeast UT and southwest CO.\n\nThis is a preliminary forecast that hinges on slight shifts in a\nstrong moisture plume impacting the region. It also attempts to\ncapture the most statistically likely outcome, despite stronger and\nweaker outlier predictions. Outdoor enthusiasts, motorists, and\nresidents of recent flood impacted areas are encouraged to remain\nabreast of the developing forecast in the coming days.","ends":"2026-10-30T15:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8fb5d34f2d13ed8ac1ea289950d1e46b096a7bdd.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8fb5d34f2d13ed8ac1ea289950d1e46b096a7bdd.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-30T06:04:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 300604 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1204 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Precipitation chances return Friday PM with quick-hitting showers and storms for the lower elevations and flurries above 10,000 feet. - Another round of 1 to 3 inches of rain for parts of southeast Utah and Southwest Colorado is possible Sunday into Monda","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-30T06:36:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-302200- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1235 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"pgs01","station_name":"Pagosa Springs","distance_km":2.6,"soil_temp_5cm_f":45.21,"soil_temp_15cm_f":48.09,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":0,"wind_speed_mph":0,"air_temp_f":32.73,"relative_humidity":0.868,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/pgs01"},"aviation":{"icao":"KPSO","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KPSO 300755Z AUTO 29002KT 10SM CLR 03/M02 A3027 RMK AO2","temp_f":37,"wind_dir":290,"wind